{"step_index":187,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-08T05:40:03Z","content":"# 🛡️ SecureChain — Software Supply Chain Security Platform\n\n## 📖 What is this Project About?\n\n**SecureChain** is a full-stack **Software Supply Chain Security Platform**. Modern software development relies heavily on open-source libraries, third-party packages, and automated CI/CD build pipelines. However, attackers increasingly target these software supply chains to inject malicious code, hijack builds, or steal secrets before software reaches production.\n\nSecureChain allows security teams, developers, and DevOps engineers to model, monitor, and defend the complete software lifecycle:\n$$\\text{Developer} \\longrightarrow \\text{Source Repo} \\longrightarrow \\text{Build Pipeline} \\longrightarrow \\text{Dependencies} \\longrightarrow \\text{Security Scan} \\longrightarrow \\text{Artifact Repository}$$\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Key Security Threats Addressed\n\n| Security Threat | Description | How SecureChain Detects & Prevents It |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Dependency Confusion Attacks** | Attackers publish malicious packages on public registries (like npm or PyPI) with names identical to internal company libraries to trick build tools into pulling the fake package. | Detects internal/private package names resolved from public registries without scope protection or private registry pinning. |\n| **Malicious Packages** | Compromised open-source packages containing backdoors, Bitcoin miners, or sabotage code (e.g., `event-stream`, `node-ipc`, `xz-utils`). | Scans dependencies against an active Threat Intelligence Feed and flags version anomalies. |\n| **Artifact Tampering** | Attackers alter compiled binaries, JARs, or Docker images in the repository after they leave the build system. | Stores SHA-256 cryptographic hashes and Cosign signature metadata; includes a live verification tool to detect checksum mismatches. |\n| **Build Compromise** | Untrusted or tampered build environments producing unverified artifacts. | Evaluates SLSA (Supply-chain Levels for Software Artifacts) build provenance data and alerts on unverified builds. |\n| **Sec\n<truncated 1729 bytes>\n`: Provide real-time security stats and severity distributions.\n\n### 2. 💻 Frontend Application (`React` + `TypeScript` + `Vite` + `Tailwind CSS`)\n* **Interactive Dashboard (`src/pages/Dashboard.tsx`)**: Displays overall risk score, critical counts, ecosystem charts, threat type breakdown (Recharts), and visual supply chain pipeline.\n* **Projects & Detail View (`src/pages/ProjectDetail.tsx`)**: Tabbed interface showing project health, dependency tree, historical scans, build logs, and registered artifacts.\n* **Scan & Findings Explorer (`src/pages/Scans.tsx`, `src/pages/Findings.tsx`)**: Real-time vulnerability table with CVSS scores, CVE references, and direct links to create remediation tasks.\n* **Remediation Tracker (`src/pages/Remediations.tsx`)**: Workflow management page to track fixes and mark security issues resolved.\n* **Artifact Verification (`src/pages/ArtifactsPage.tsx`)**: Interactive tool to verify SHA-256 checksums against stored records.\n* **Threat Intel Feed (`src/pages/ThreatIntelPage.tsx`)**: Management interface to view and add custom malicious package signatures.\n* **Risk Report Generator (`src/pages/Report.tsx`)**: Executive report view with severity radar charts and downloadable CycloneDX SBOM JSON.\n\n### 3. 🧪 Demo Data & Automated Seeding (`seed_data.py`)\n* Created a database seeder with realistic test projects:\n  * `payment-gateway-service`: Contains real-world malicious dependencies (`event-stream@3.3.6`, sabotaged `colors`), dependency confusion targets (`internal-utils@99.0.0`), and an exposed AWS key in build logs.\n  * `auth-identity-service`: Contains vulnerable Django/aiohttp versions and unverified build provenance.\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 How to Run the Platform\n\n### Option A: Local Development\n1. **Backend**:\n   ```bash\n   cd backend\n   python main.py\n   ```\n   * Access API docs at `http://127.0.0.1:8001/docs`\n\n2. **Frontend**:\n   ```bash\n   cd frontend\n   npm run dev\n   ```\n   * Access Web UI at `http://localhost:5173`\n\n### Option B: Docker Compose\n```bash\ndocker-compose up --build\n```","truncated_fields":["content"]}
